{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And et.</b> Know that the masters of the holy language preserve verbs in their patterns across all binyanim, and they are not particular to preserve the names of people. Therefore do not wonder that he said גרשום on account of גר שם (\"I was a stranger there\"), for behold Cain (Genesis 4:1) on account of קניתי (\"I have acquired\"), and it is not correct grammatically, and harder than this is Noah (ibid. 5:29) from the root ינחמנו (\"he will comfort us\"), and sometimes they invert the name, like Hushim (ibid. 46:23) who is Shaharaim (Numbers 26:42) [n. The identity of the inverted-name example is textually difficult; read as given.], and Jabez is so called (1 Chronicles 4:9) on account of his mother bearing him in pain. Also they may drop a letter from it, like Maacah who is Micah, and the opposite, Job (Genesis 46:13) who is Jashub (Numbers 26:24). And many said that so too Samuel (1 Samuel 1:20) is from the root שאלתיהו (\"I asked him\"), and it seems right to me that the shureq stands in place of the cholam, like תֹחוּ (1 Samuel 1:1), Tohu (1 Chronicles 6:19) [n. Reading תיח as a corrupted form of תחו/תוח, consistent with the shureq/cholam argument.], and Zuph (1 Samuel 1:1) and Zuphai (1 Chronicles 6:11), Nun (below, 33:11) and Nun (1 Chronicles 7:27). And so Samuel is from the name El, \"and I called him by the name El,\" for they gave to her as she asked. And do not wonder how a person's name is called by the name El, for behold like it is צוּרִי שדי (Numbers 7:36?) [n. The citation as printed does not correspond to any verse containing \"צורי שדי\"; the reference is unresolved.], and many like this. And in my opinion, so too the name of the altar that Moses built — יהוה נסי (above, 17:15), and likewise the name of the Messiah — יהוה צדקנו (Jeremiah 23:6). And the Gaon [Saadia] said that the Name is joined with the word יקראו, and the name of the Messiah is צדקנו. But this follows from the Masoretic accents, which place the stress on the word יקראו, for there is a great difference between ויקרא בשם ה' (Genesis 12:8) written of Abraham, where the stress is on ויקרא, and ויקרא בשם ה' (below, 34:5) written in the book of Moses, as I will explain in its place."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:34.909491084Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}